<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Normals: equation of the normal at a given point (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The <strong>normal</strong> to a curve at a point is the line through that point that is <em>perpendicular</em> to the tangent there.</p>
<p>Find the equation of the normal to</p>
\[ f(x) = {@fx@} \]
<p>at the point where \( x = {@aa@} \).</p>
<p><strong>Step 1.</strong> The gradient of the <em>tangent</em> at \( x = {@aa@} \):</p>
<div>\( m_{\text{tangent}} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p><strong>Step 2.</strong> The gradient of the <em>normal</em> (give an exact value, e.g. <code>-1/4</code>):</p>
<div>\( m_{\text{normal}} = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p><strong>Step 3.</strong> The equation of the normal:</p>
<div>\( y = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1.</strong> \( f'(x) = {@dfx@} \), so the tangent has gradient \( f'({@aa@}) = {@mt@} \).</p>
<p><strong>Step 2.</strong> Perpendicular gradients multiply to \( -1 \), so the normal has gradient</p>
\[ m_{\text{normal}} = -\frac{1}{f'({@aa@})} = -\frac{1}{{@mt@}} = {@mn@}. \]
<p>(Turn the tangent gradient upside down <em>and</em> change its sign.)</p>
<p><strong>Step 3.</strong> The point is \( ({@aa@},\ f({@aa@})) = ({@aa@},\ {@fa@}) \). Using \( y = m(x - a) + f(a) \):</p>
\[
\begin{align*}
y &= {@mn@}\left(x - ({@aa@})\right) + ({@fa@}) \\
  &= {@nl@}.
\end{align*}
\]
<p>Check: \( {@mt@} \times \left({@mn@}\right) = -1 \), so the normal is perpendicular to the tangent.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
